HST: HTML pasted from a web page reads as normal text on the Writer page

Browsers put the computed colours of the page on the clipboard. Copied from a page in dark mode
(Facebook, but any site), the text came with rgb(226, 229, 233) on dark bands of rgb(37, 39, 40),
and "background-color: transparent" on links, names and time stamps removed the band below them:
near-white text on the white page. When HTML is pasted or inserted into an existing document
(not when an HTML file is opened), dark neutral bands (luminance at most 0.1, channels at most 64
apart) are dropped, in paragraphs, characters, sections and table cells, and a text colour (CSS
color, <font color>, <body text/link/vlink>) is dropped when its WCAG contrast against the
background the text gets in Writer (own background, open character background ignoring
transparent, paragraph background, page) is below 1.6:1, or below 3:1 for text whose dark band
was dropped. Readable colours, bold, italic and links stay; a light-mode copy is unchanged.
Logic in sw/source/filter/html/hsthtmlpaste.cxx; hooks in InsertAttrs/InsertAttr (htmlctxt.cxx),
NewDivision (htmlsect.cxx), CreateBrushItem (htmltab.cxx) and the body options (htmlgrin.cxx).

Test: CppunitTest_sw_hst_html_paste.

